subprocess.run(['python3','-I','-B','write_lane.py','search-response-2.json'],input="\"Selecta Mathematica (2022) 28:44 (https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10520534)\\nciteturn1search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Spherical Schubert varieties and pattern avoidance ... In this paper we resolve a conjecture of Hodges–Yong [7], thereby clas-\\nSelecta Mathematica (2022) 28:44\\nhttps://doi.org/10.1007/s00029-022-00760-8\\nSelecta Mathematica\\nNew Series\\nSpherical Schubert varieties and pattern avoidance\\nChristian Gaetz1\\nAccepted: 4 January 2022 / Published online: 31 January 2022\\n© The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2022\\nAbstract\\nA normal variety X is called H-spherical for the action of the complex reductive group\\nH if it contains a dense orbit of some Borel subgroup of H. We resolve a conjecture of\\nHodges–Yong by showing that their spherical permutations are characterized by per-\\nmutation pattern avoidance. Together with results of Gao–Hodges–Yong this implies\\nthat the sphericality of a Schubert variety Xw with respect to the largest possible Levi\\nsubgroup is characterized by this same pattern avoidance condition.\\nKeywords Spherical variety · Schubert variety · Permutation pattern avoidance\\nMathematics Subject Classiﬁcation 05E14 · 14M15 · 05A05\\n1 Introduction\\n1.1 Spherical varieties\\nFollowing [3, 11], a normal variety X is called H-spherical for the action of the com-\\nplex reductive group H if it contains a dense orbit of some Borel subgroup of H.\\nImportant examples of spherical varieties include projective and afﬁne toric varieties,\\ncomplexiﬁcations of symmetric spaces, and ﬂag varieties (see Perrin’s survey [13]).\\nProducing families of examples of and classifying spherical varieties is of signiﬁcant\\ninterest [10]. In this paper we resolve a conjecture of Hodges–Yong [7], thereby clas-\\nsifying (maximally) spherical Schubert varieties by a permutation pattern avoidance\\ncondition.\\nThe author was supported by a National Science Foundation Graduate Research Fellowship under Grant\\nNo. 1122374.\\nB Christian Gaetz\\ngaetz@math.harvard.edu\\n1\\nDepartment of Mathematics, Harvard University, Cambridge, MA 02138, USA--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nJournal of Lie Theory (https://www.heldermann-verlag.de/jlt/jlt32/jlt1883-b.pdf)\\nciteturn1search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Reuven Hodges and Alexander Yong ... We evidence the conjecture, employing the combinatorics of Demazure modules, ... Key Words: Schubert varieties, spherical varieties, key polynomials, split symmetry.\\nJournal of Lie Theory\\nVolume 32 (2022) 447–474\\n© 2022 Heldermann Verlag\\nCoxeter Combinatorics and Spherical Schubert Geometry\\nReuven Hodges and Alexander Yong\\nCommunicated by R. Avdeev\\nAbstract.\\nFor a finite Coxeter system and a subset of its diagram nodes, we define spher-\\nical elements (a generalization of Coxeter elements). Conjecturally, for Weyl groups, spherical\\nelements index Schubert varieties in a flag manifold G/B that are spherical for the action of a\\nLevi subgroup. We evidence the conjecture, employing the combinatorics of Demazure modules,\\nand work of R. Avdeev and A. Petukhov, M. Can and R. Hodges, R. Hodges and V. Lakshmibai,\\nP. Karuppuchamy, P. Magyar and J. Weyman and A. Zelevinsky, N. Perrin, J. Stembridge, and\\nB. Tenner. In type A, we establish connections with the key polynomials of A. Lascoux and M.-\\nP. Schützenberger, multiplicity-freeness, and split-symmetry in algebraic combinatorics. Thereby,\\nwe invoke theorems of A. Kohnert, V. Reiner and M. Shimozono, and C. Ross and A. Yong.\\nMathematics Subject Classification: Primary 14M15; secondary 05E05, 05E10, 14L30.\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n2  \\nYibo Gao, Reuven Hodges, Alexander Yong\\n\\n**Def (https://ayong.web.illinois.edu/GHY.FPSAC.2022.pdf)\\nciteturn1search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.6 years ago; Yibo Gao, Reuven Hodges, Alexander Yong ... In [10, Conjecture 3.2], a conjectural combinatorial classification of \\\\(L_I\\\\)-spherical Schubert varieties was stated. In [9] (see Section 3) it was proved that said conjecture is equivalent to the following theorem of *ibid.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nis the number of *inversions* of \\\\(w\\\\).\\n\\nA *parabolic subgroup* \\\\(W_I\\\\) of \\\\(W\\\\) is the subgroup generated by a subset \\\\(I \\\\subset S\\\\). Furthermore, a *standard Coxeter element* \\\\(c \\\\in W_I\\\\) is the product of the elements of \\\\(I\\\\) listed in some order. Let \\\\(w_0(I)\\\\) denote the longest element of \\\\(W_I\\\\).\\n\\n**Definition 1.2** ([9, Definition 1.1]). Let \\\\(w \\\\in W\\\\) and fix \\\\(I \\\\subset J(w)\\\\). Then \\\\(w\\\\) is \\\\(I\\\\)-spherical if \\\\(w_0(I)w\\\\) is a standard Coxeter element for some parabolic subgroup \\\\(W_I\\\\) of \\\\(W\\\\).\\n\\nIn [10, Conjecture 3.2], a conjectural combinatorial classification of \\\\(L_I\\\\)-spherical Schubert varieties was stated. In [9] (see Section 3) it was proved that said conjecture is equivalent to the following theorem of *ibid.*\\n\\n**Theorem 1.3** ([9, Theorem 1.5]). Let \\\\(w \\\\in \\\\mathfrak{S}_n\\\\) and \\\\(I \\\\subset J(w)\\\\). \\\\(X_w \\\\subset GL_n/B\\\\) is \\\\(L_I\\\\)-spherical if and only if \\\\(w\\\\) is \\\\(I\\\\)-spherical.\\n\\nThe proof uses the theory of *Demazure characters* and their manifestation in algebraic combinatorics, the *key polynomials*. One of the results used is a classification of *multiplicity-free key polynomials* [11, Theorem 1.1]. This is explained in Section 2.\\n\\nLet us also mention some other related results. Theorem 1.3 is used in C. Gaetz’s [8], which proves [10, Conjecture 3.8]. Consequently, this gives a pattern avoidance criterion for maximally spherical Schubert varieties [8, Theorem 1.4, Corollary 1.5]. Earlier work of D. Brewster-R. Hodges-A. Yong [3] proved a weaker numerical assertion, that\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\lim_{n \\\\to \\\\infty} \\\\Pr[w \\\\in \\\\mathfrak{S}_n, w is\\\\ I\\\\text{-spherical for some } I \\\\subset J(w)] = 0,\\n\\\\]\\n\\nas well as its geometric counterpart\\n\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\lim_{n \\\\to \\\\infty} \\\\Pr[w \\\\in \\\\mathfrak{S}_n, X_w \\\\text{ is } L_I\\\\text{-spherical for some } I \\\\subset J(w)] = 0.\\n\\\\]--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nCoxeter Combinatorics and Spherical Schubert Geometry (https://jolt.centre-mersenne.org/articles/10.5802/jolt.1238/)\\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; Coxeter Combinatorics and Spherical Schubert Geometry\\n\\nReuven Hodges^{1}; Alexander Yong^{2} ... We evidence the conjecture, employing the combinatorics of Demazure modules, and work of R.\\n\\nCoxeter Combinatorics and Spherical Schubert Geometry\\n\\nReuven Hodges^{1}; Alexander Yong^{2}\\n\\n^{1}Dept. of Mathematics, University of California at San Diego, La Jolla, U.S.A.\\n^{2}Dept. of Mathematics, University of Illinois, Urbana-Champaign, U.S.A.\\n\\nJournal of Lie Theory, Volume 32 (2022) no. 2, pp. 447-474\\n\\nFor a finite Coxeter system and a subset of its diagram nodes, we define spherical elements (a generalization of Coxeter elements). Conjecturally, for Weyl groups, spherical elements index Schubert varieties in a flag manifold G/B that are spherical for the action of a Levi subgroup. We evidence the conjecture, employing the combinatorics of Demazure modules, and work of R. Avdeev and A. Petukhov, M. Can and R. Hodges, R. Hodges and V. Lakshmibai, P. Karuppuchamy, P. Magyar and J. Weyman and A. Zelevinsky, N. Perrin, J. Stembridge, and B. Tenner. In type A, we establish connections with the key polynomials of A. Lascoux and M.-P. Schützenberger, multiplicity-freeness, and split-symmetry in algebraic combinatorics. Thereby, we invoke theorems of A. Kohnert, V. Reiner and M. Shimozono, and C. Ross and A. Yong.\\n\\nZbl arXiv\\n\\nDOI: 10.5802/jolt.1238\\n\\nClassification: 14M15, 05E05, 05E10, 14L30\\nKeywords: Schubert varieties, spherical varieties, key polynomials, split symmetry\\n\\nAuthor's affiliations:\\n\\nReuven Hodges  ^{ 1 } ; Alexander Yong  ^{ 2 }\\n\\n^{1} Dept. of Mathematics, University of California at San Diego, La Jolla, U.S.A.\\n^{2} Dept. of Mathematics, University of Illinois, Urbana-Champaign, U.S.A.\\n    \\n    Reuven Hodges; Alexander Yong. Coxeter Combinatorics and Spherical Schubert Geometry. Journal of Lie Theory, Volume 32 (2022) no. 2, pp. 447-474. doi: 10.5802/jolt.1238\\n    \\n    @article{JOLT_2022_32_2_a6,\\n         author = {Reuven Hodges and Alexander Yong},\\n         title = {Coxeter {Combinatorics} and {Spherical} {Schubert} {Geometry}},\\n         journal = {Journal of Lie Theory},\\n         pages = {447--474},\\n         year = {2022},\\n         volume = {32},\\n         number = {2},\\n         doi = {10.5802/jolt.1238},\\n         zbl = {1486.14070},\\n         url = {https://jolt.centre-mersenne.org/articles/10.5802/jolt.1238/}\\n    }\\n    \\n    TY  - JOUR\\n    AU  - Reuven Hodges\\n    AU  - Alexander Yong\\n    TI  - Coxeter Combinatorics and Spherical Schubert Geometry\\n    JO  - Journal of Lie Theory\\n    PY  - 2022\\n    SP  - 447\\n    EP  - 474\\n    VL  - 32\\n    IS  - 2\\n    UR  - https://jolt.centre-mersenne.org/articles/10.5802/jolt.1238/\\n    DO  - 10.5802/jolt.1238\\n    ID  - JOLT_2022_32_2_a6\\n\\nISSN : 0949-5932\\ne-ISSN : 2981-2917--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nLevi-spherical varieties and Demazure characters (https://fpsac2024.rub.de/public/submitted_posters/hodges.pdf)\\nciteturn1search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Yibo Gao, Reuven Hodges, Alexander Yong ... natorial classification of Levi-spherical Schubert ... | Classification This theorem resolves the main conjecture of [3].\\nLevi-spherical varieties and Demazure characters\\nYibo Gao, Reuven Hodges, Alexander Yong\\nDepartment of Mathematics, University of Kansas\\nLevi subgroup actions\\nAbstract\\nWe prove a short, root-system uniform, combi-\\nnatorial classification of Levi-spherical Schubert\\nvarieties for any generalized flag variety G/B\\nof finite Lie type. We apply this to the study\\nof multiplicity-free decompositions of Demazure\\nmodules and their characters.\\nClassification | --- | --- Classification | Classification Theorem 1 ([2]) | Classification | Classification | Classification Fix w ∈ W and I ⊆ D (w). X is L -spherical L w I if and only if w is I-spherical. | Classification This theorem resolves the main conjecture of [3]. In [1], this theorem was established for G = GL . n |Schubert varieties\\nOur choice of T and B determine the root system\\nΦ and simple roots ∆= {α1, . . . , αr}, respectively.\\nThe Weyl group W of G, is generated by the set of\\nsimple reflections {si:=sαi|αi∈∆}. For I ⊆∆, WI\\nis the subgroup of W generated by {sαi |αi∈I}.\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nClassification of Levi-spherical Schubert varieties (https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.10101)\\nciteturn1academia16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.6 years ago; Title: Classification of Levi-spherical Schubert varieties\\nAuthors: Yibo Gao, Reuven Hodges, Alexander Yong ... This establishes a conjecture of the latter two authors, and a new formulation in terms of standard Coxeter elements.\\nTitle: Classification of Levi-spherical Schubert varieties\\nAuthors: Yibo Gao, Reuven Hodges, Alexander Yong\\nDate: Tue Apr 20 16:33:54 2021\\n\\nA Schubert variety in the complete flag manifold $GL_n/B$ is Levi-spherical if the action of a Borel subgroup in a Levi subgroup of a standard parabolic has a dense orbit. We give a combinatorial classification of these Schubert varieties. This establishes a conjecture of the latter two authors, and a new formulation in terms of standard Coxeter elements. Our proof uses the theory of key polynomials (type A Demazure module characters).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nCoxeter combinatorics and spherical Schubert geometry (https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.09238)\\nciteturn1academia17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.3 years ago; Title: Coxeter combinatorics and spherical Schubert geometry\\nAuthors: Reuven Hodges, Alexander Yong ... We evidence the conjecture, employing the combinatorics of Demazure modules, and work of R.\\nTitle: Coxeter combinatorics and spherical Schubert geometry\\nAuthors: Reuven Hodges, Alexander Yong\\nDate: Fri Jul 17 21:15:08 2020\\n\\nFor a finite Coxeter system and a subset of its diagram nodes, we define spherical elements (a generalization of Coxeter elements). Conjecturally, for Weyl groups, spherical elements index Schubert varieties in a flag manifold G/B that are spherical for the action of a Levi subgroup. We evidence the conjecture, employing the combinatorics of Demazure modules, and work of R. Avdeev-A. Petukhov, M. Can-R. Hodges, R. Hodges-V. Lakshmibai, P. Karuppuchamy, P. Magyar-J. Weyman-A. Zelevinsky, N. Perrin, J. Stembridge, and B. Tenner. In type A, we establish connections with the key polynomials of A. Lascoux- M.-P. Sch\\\\\\\"utzenberger, multiplicity-freeness, and split-symmetry in algebraic combinatorics. Thereby, we invoke theorems of A. Kohnert, V. Reiner-M. Shimozono, and C. Ross-A. Yong.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nSpherical Schubert varieties and pattern avoidance (https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.03264)\\nciteturn1academia18 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.6 years ago; We resolve a conjecture of Hodges--Yong by showing that their spherical permutations are characterized by permutation pattern avoidance. Together with results of Gao--Hodges--Yong this implies that the sphericality of a Schubert variety $X_w$ with respect to the largest possible Levi subgroup is characterized by this same pattern avoidance condition.\\nTitle: Spherical Schubert varieties and pattern avoidance\\nAuthors: Christian Gaetz\\nDate: Wed Apr  7 17:11:26 2021\\n\\nA normal variety $X$ is called $H$-spherical for the action of the complex reductive group $H$ if it contains a dense orbit of some Borel subgroup of $H$. We resolve a conjecture of Hodges--Yong by showing that their spherical permutations are characterized by permutation pattern avoidance. Together with results of Gao--Hodges--Yong this implies that the sphericality of a Schubert variety $X_w$ with respect to the largest possible Levi subgroup is characterized by this same pattern avoidance condition.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nBinomial edge ideals of regularity $3$ (https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.09002)\\nciteturn1academia19 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.4 years ago; Authors: Sara Saeedi Madani, Dariush Kiani ... In particular we disprove a conjecture in \\\\cite{CDI} on the regularity of weakly closed graphs.\\nTitle: Binomial edge ideals of regularity $3$\\nAuthors: Sara Saeedi Madani, Dariush Kiani\\nDate: Tue Jun 27 18:34:10 2017\\n\\nLet $J_G$ be the binomial edge ideal of a graph $G$. We characterize all graphs whose binomial edge ideals, as well as their initial ideals, have regularity $3$. Consequently we characterize all graphs $G$ such that $J_G$ is extremal Gorenstein. Indeed, these characterizations are consequences of an explicit formula we obtain for the regularity of the binomial edge ideal of the join product of two graphs. Finally, by using our regularity formula, we discuss some open problems in the literature. In particular we disprove a conjecture in \\\\cite{CDI} on the regularity of weakly closed graphs.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nCOMPLETING THE REDUCED WORD CRITERION IN FINITE WEYL TYPES (https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/bulletin-of-the-australian-mathematical-society/article/abs/completing-the-reduced-word-criterion-in-finite-weyl-types/2CAAE1F5FAE59AA9517A12F70925EC3E)\\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 weeks ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Hodges and Yong [‘Coxeter combinatorics and spherical Schubert geometry’, J. ... Gao, Y., Hodges, R. and Yong, A., ‘Classifying Levi-spherical Schubert varieties’, Sém.\\n\\n# COMPLETING THE REDUCED WORD CRITERION IN FINITE WEYL TYPES\\n\\nPart of: Special aspects of infinite or finite groups Special varieties\\n\\nPublished online by Cambridge University Press:  19 October 2026\\n\\nYINDONG JIN [Opens in a new window]  and\\n\\nQIAN ZHAO [Opens in a new window]\\n\\n## Abstract\\n\\nHodges and Yong [‘Coxeter combinatorics and spherical Schubert geometry’, J. Lie Theory 32 (2022), 447–474] proposed a reduced word criterion for Levi spherical Schubert varieties. It was proved in type A and in rank two, while counterexamples were found in types 𝐷4 and 𝐹4. A later standard Coxeter element criterion settled Levi sphericity in every finite Lie type, but whether the original criterion remained valid in types B and C was still open. We prove that the two criteria agree in both families. These are the last unresolved infinite Weyl families. Consequently, the original reduced word criterion characterises Levi sphericity for every Weyl group element and every allowed Levi subgroup precisely in types A, B, C and 𝐺2. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nMin (https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~slc/wpapers/FPSAC2026/98.html)\\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; L-spherical Schubert varieties in complete flag varieties were treated independently by Gao-Hodges-Yong and Can-Saha.\\n##### Séminaire Lotharingien de Combinatoire, 95B.98 (2026), 12 pp.\\n\\n# Jaewon Min\\n\\n# When is a Schubert Variety Spherical?\\n\\nAbstract. Let L be a Levi subgroup of a general linear group acting on a Schubert variety. If there is a dense open orbit of a Borel subgroup, the Schubert variety is L-spherical, generalizing the concept of toric varieties. Hodges and Lakshmibai classified L-spherical Schubert varieties in Grassmannians. L-spherical Schubert varieties in complete flag varieties were treated independently by Gao-Hodges-Yong and Can-Saha. In this paper, we generalize these classifications to Schubert varieties in partial flag varieties of type A. This is achieved by applying Lakshmibai-Seshadri paths forming crystal graphs of Demazure modules, which has a root-system uniform framework.\\n\\nReceived: November 15, 2025. Accepted: February 15, 2026. Final version: April 1, 2026.\\n\\n* * *\\n\\nThe following versions are available:\\n\\n  * PDF (219 K)\\n  * TeX version--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nHorospherical Schubert varieties (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40879-026-00924-2)\\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; We also introduce a new class of spherical varieties, which we call doubly-spherical varieties, and show that all nearly toric Schubert varieties belong to this family. ... Our analysis led us to consider a new family of spherical varieties and provide characterizations for several other significant families of spherical varieties in the context of Schubert varieties.\\n\\n# Horospherical Schubert varieties\\n\\n  * Research Article\\n  * Open access\\n  * Published: 31 August 2026\\n\\n  * Volume 12, article number 50 (2026)\\n  * Cite this article\\n\\nYou have full access to this open access article\\n\\nDownload PDF\\n\\nSave article\\n\\nView saved research\\n\\nEuropean Journal of Mathematics Aims and scope Submit manuscript\\n\\nHorospherical Schubert varieties\\n\\nDownload PDF\\n\\n  * Mahir Bilen Can^{1},\\n  * S. Senthamarai Kannan^{2} &\\n  * Pinakinath Saha^{3} \\n\\n  * 63 Accesses\\n\\n  * Explore all metrics\\n\\n## Abstract\\n\\nThe algebraic group structure of stabilizers of points in Schubert varieties is investigated. We show that these stabilizers are always strongly solvable algebraic groups and discuss their connectedness properties. Furthermore, we provide a complete characterization of horospherical Schubert varieties and prove that every horospherical Schubert variety is nonsingular. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nApplications of homogeneous fiber bundles to the Schubert varieties (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/374057895_Applications_of_homogeneous_fiber_bundles_to_the_Schubert_varieties)\\nciteturn1search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.1 years ago; Crawled: 6 months ago; We give a short proof of a conjecture of Gao, Hodges, and Yong for deciding when a Schubert variety is spherical with respect to an action of a Levi subgroup.\\n\\n# Applications of homogeneous fiber bundles to the Schubert varieties\\n\\n  * September 2023\\n  * Geometriae Dedicata 217(6)\\n\\nDOI:10.1007/s10711-023-00839-2\\n\\nAuthors:\\n\\nMahir Bilen Can\\n\\nMahir Bilen Can\\n\\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\\n\\nPinaki Saha\\n\\nPinaki Saha\\n\\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\\n\\n## Abstract and Figures\\n\\nThis article explores the relationship between Schubert varieties and equivariant embeddings, using the framework of homogeneous fiber bundles over flag varieties. We show that the homogenous fiber bundles obtained from Bott–Samelson–Demazure–Hansen varieties are always toroidal. Furthermore, we identify the wonderful varieties among them. We give a short proof of a conjecture of Gao, Hodges, and Yong for deciding when a Schubert variety is spherical with respect to an action of a Levi subgroup. By using BP-decompositions, we obtain a characterization of the smooth spherical Schubert varieties. using the framework of homogeneous ﬁber bundles over ﬂag varieties. We show that the\\n\\nhomogenous ﬁber bundles obtained from Bott–Samelson–Demazure–Hansen varieties are\\n\\nalways toroidal. Furthermore, we identify the wonderful varieties among them. We give a\\n\\nshort proof of a conjecture of Gao, Hodges, and Yong for deciding when a Schubert variety is\\n\\nspherical with respect to an action of a Levi subgroup. By using BP-decompositions, we obtain\\n\\na characterization of the smooth spherical Schubert varieties. Among the other applications\\n\\nof our results are: (1) a characterization of the spherical Bott–Samelson–Demazure–Hansen\\n\\nvarieties, (2) an alternative proof of the fact that, in type A, every singular Schubert variety of\\n\\ntorus complexity 1 is a spherical Schubert variety, and (3) a proof of the fact that, for simply\\n\\nlaced algebraic groups of adjoint type, every spherical G-Schubert variety is locally rigid,\\n\\nthat is to say, the ﬁrst cohomology of its tangent sheaf vanishes.\\n\\nKeywords Wonderful varieties ·Spherical varieties ·Toroidal varieties ·Schubert varieties ·\\n\\nG-Schubert varieties ·BSDH-varieties ·G-BSDH-varieties ·Automorphism groups ·\\n\\nTangent sheaves\\n\\n1 Introduction\\n\\nLet G be a connected reductive group deﬁned over an algebraically closed ﬁeld. Let X be a\\n\\nnormal G-variety. If a Borel subgroup B of G has an open orbit in X,then X is said to be a\\n\\nspherical G-variety. This conventional deﬁnition of a spherical variety requires a reductive\\n\\nmahirbilencan@gmail.com\\n\\n1 Tulane University, New Orleans, LA, USA\\n\\n2 Indian Institute of Science, Bengaluru, India--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\nRegularity bound of generalized binomial edge ideal of graphs (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0021869319306246)\\nciteturn1search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.7 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; Indeed, we prove Saeedi Madani-Kiani regularity upper bound conjectu